**Planned.** Nothing on this page is built, installed, bought or run yet. Every test below is a plan, to be filmed when it happens.

**Whole task:** after the whole arm moves, drive PAROL6 from a robot-learning library. Then record what a person shows it, and train it to repeat the task.

## What LeRobot is

**LeRobot** is Hugging Face's open-source Python library for robot learning ([huggingface.co/lerobot](https://huggingface.co/lerobot)). Its licence is [Apache-2.0](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot/blob/e581c1d5701f846788662079d4ac08835c96def9/LICENSE). This page reads version 0.7.1, at commit [e581c1d](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot/tree/e581c1d5701f846788662079d4ac08835c96def9).

It gives the whole loop of **imitation learning**: a robot learns a task from recorded demonstrations.

- **Teleoperation:** a person drives the robot live from another device. A **leader arm** is an arm the person moves; the robot follows it. A phone or a gamepad also works.
- **Recording:** `lerobot-record` saves each demonstration. An **episode** is one recorded demonstration, with joint angles and camera video, in LeRobot's dataset format ([dataset docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/lerobot-dataset-v3)).
- **Training:** `lerobot-train` fits a policy to the episodes. A policy is a model that turns camera frames and joint angles into the next actions. 31 policy packages ship.
- **Running:** `lerobot-rollout` runs a trained policy on the robot.

Two policies matter first:

- **ACT** is a policy with a ResNet-18 image backbone and a transformer over the camera features and joints. It predicts chunks of actions ([ACT docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/act)).
- **SmolVLA** is a 450M vision-language-action model, fine-tuned from `smolvla_base`. It takes a text prompt with the cameras and joints ([SmolVLA docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/smolvla)).

## Why PAROL6 needs its own driver

LeRobot's motor buses are for smart servos: Feetech and Dynamixel on a serial bus, and Damiao and Robstride on CAN. PAROL6 has step/dir stepper motors behind its own control board. At this commit, no step/dir stepper arm ships with LeRobot.

For that case, the docs say to write your own communication wrapper ([Bring your own hardware](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/integrate_hardware)). The driver is a `Robot` class ([`robot.py`](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot/blob/e581c1d5701f846788662079d4ac08835c96def9/src/lerobot/robots/robot.py)) in its own pip package.

- LeRobot imports any installed package whose name starts with `lerobot_robot_` when a command starts ([`import_utils.py`](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot/blob/e581c1d5701f846788662079d4ac08835c96def9/src/lerobot/utils/import_utils.py)).
- So our package, `lerobot_robot_parol6`, needs no fork of LeRobot. The commands then accept `--robot.type=parol6`.

## Why the driver talks to `parol6-server`

Our plan is a driver that is a client of the official **parol6-server**. The server is part of the [PAROL6 Python API](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/02-python-api/). It owns the USB serial port and sends the board one packet every 10 ms.

The board itself is simple. Per its [firmware](https://github.com/Source-Robotics/PAROL6-Desktop-robot-arm/blob/e2169c163244d647739cc34fc5da15c24b042a61/PAROL6%20control%20board%20main%20software/src/main.cpp):

- it repeats the last command it received, with no timeout of its own;
- it checks no joint limits;
- it only reads and reports the E-stop.

The [server](https://github.com/PCrnjak/PAROL6-python-API/tree/604d1ec8b4c71be119e75f1506d4cc388322230c) already holds the safety logic:

- the E-stop latch, which disables the board and refuses motion until `reset()`;
- the guard that refuses streamed and planned moves until [homing](/lore/m3-whole-arm) is done;
- the joint limits;
- the 100 Hz loop to the board;
- a brake to a hold when a client stream goes quiet for 0.25 s.

A driver on the serial port would have to rebuild all of that. Only one program can own the port, so LeRobot and the commander cannot both connect.

The server also runs as a simulator behind the same API. So the driver can be written and tested before the arm moves.

<Diagram alt="The planned chain: a leader drives LeRobot, LeRobot calls our driver, the driver calls parol6-server, and the server drives the board. The E-stop reports to the server; the mains switch cuts the board's power.">

```text
  leader: phone, gamepad or SO-101 arm
     │
  LeRobot: record, train, run
     │
  lerobot_robot_parol6, our driver:
  clip, cap each step, check homing
     │  UDP
  parol6-server, official:
  E-stop latch, limits, 100 Hz, brake
     │  USB serial
  control board ◀── E-stop
     │
  power supply ◀─── mains switch
```

</Diagram>

**What to notice:** the E-stop works only while the server runs. The mains switch works always.

## The milestones, L0 to L5

Each L milestone is a whole task with its own test, in the same shape as [the M milestones](/lore/milestones). All the tests are our design.

### L0 (optional, before the arm moves): the driver in simulation

- **Whole task:** write `lerobot_robot_parol6` and drive the official simulator through it.
- **Depends on:** none of M1 to M7. It needs Python 3.12; on a Mac, macOS 15.
- **Test**, with `parol6-server` in simulator mode:
  1. `connect()` succeeds. The observation lists the six joints, the gripper and the cameras.
  2. A 30 Hz loop of `send_action` moves joint 6 by +10°. The observation lands within 0.5°.
  3. A target past a joint limit comes back clipped in the returned action.
  4. Kill the client mid-stream. The server's status shows the stream braked to a hold.
  5. With `expect_simulator=true`, the driver refuses a server that reports real hardware.

### L1 (the week M3 passes): connect, read, jog safely, stop

- **Whole task:** LeRobot connects to the real arm through `parol6-server`, reads all six joints and makes small, slow, limited moves. Every stop path works.
- **Depends on:** [M3](/lore/m3-whole-arm), for homing, [jogging](/lore/m3-whole-arm) every joint the right way, and the [witness marks](/lore/m3-whole-arm). Also the mains-switch habit from [M1](/lore/m1-bench-it).
- **Parts:** none new. One webcam is optional.
- **Test**, on camera, with a hand on the E-stop and the mains switch in reach:
  1. Connect to a homed arm. The observation matches the commander's angles to 0.1°.
  2. Connect to an unhomed arm. The driver refuses to move it and says why. It homes only when a person confirms.
  3. The arm does not lunge on connect and does not drop on disconnect.
  4. Jog each joint +5° at 10% speed, one joint at a time. Each lands on its witness marks.
  5. Out-of-range targets are clipped. A 30° jump command is capped to the step limit.
  6. Press the E-stop mid-move. Motion stops and `send_action` raises an error. After release, nothing moves until a person calls `reset()`.
  7. Kill the LeRobot process mid-move. The server brakes to a hold within about 0.25 s, plus the deceleration.
  8. Joel explains, unprompted, why a pulled USB cable is not covered by any of this, and reaches for the mains switch.

### L2: teleoperation and a first dataset

- **Whole task:** drive the arm from a leader, record 10 episodes of a simple reach-and-touch task with two fixed cameras, and replay one.
- **Depends on:** L1. Also [M4](/lore/m4-pick-and-place) if the gripper is in the action; otherwise the gripper is held.
- **Parts:** two cameras, a mount and one leader. See the cost section.
- **Test:**
  1. `lerobot-teleoperate` runs with the chosen leader at 30 Hz for 2 minutes, with no brake events.
  2. `lerobot-record` writes a dataset of 10 episodes. The visualizer shows both cameras in sync with the joint plots.
  3. `lerobot-replay` of episode 0 reaches the same touch point within 2 mm, by feeler or dial gauge, the M4 method.

### L3: the first learned policy (ACT), against M4's script

- **Whole task:** record 50 demonstrations of M4's pick-and-place, train ACT on them, then run it.
- **Depends on:** L2 and [M4](/lore/m4-pick-and-place), for the gripper and the nests.
- **Compute:** a CUDA GPU with 8 GB or more for about an hour, or an Apple silicon Mac overnight.
- **Test:** 20 trials from a fresh object pose each time, with an operator at the E-stop. Record the success count beside M4's scripted 20-cycle result. Joel explains one failure from the camera view.

L3 overlaps M4. A learned pick-and-place is the same whole task as M4's scripted one, so M4's loop becomes the baseline.

### L4: language and a VLA (SmolVLA), compared with ACT

- **Whole task:** fine-tune `smolvla_base` on the L3 data plus one new task with a text prompt. Run both tasks by prompt.
- **Depends on:** L3.
- **Compute:** a CUDA GPU with 16 GB or more, or Hugging Face Jobs in the cloud. [Remote inference](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/remote_inference) runs the model on a GPU computer if the arm's own computer is too slow.
- **Test:** 10 trials per task. Report the success rate and the inference latency beside ACT's. Write down which one we keep and why.

### L5: a learned synth move

- **Whole task:** from demonstrations, press a named skiff button or turn a knob to noon. [M5](/lore/m5-touch-the-skiff)'s registration is the setup.
- **Depends on:** M5. Also [M6](/lore/m6-patch-a-cable), if a policy is to try a patch.
- **Test:** the policy passes the M5 done check. That is 10 of 10 button presses, or a knob turned to noon as read on a CV output. A scripted fallback stays one key away.

## What it costs

Prices were checked on 2026-11-11. Nothing is bought. Prices from a search extract can be stale, so check them again before buying.

- **L0 and L1:** no new parts. The arm from M3, a computer and free software are enough. One webcam is optional, to prove the camera path.
- **L2:** about $125 with a phone as the leader. The phone costs nothing if you have one ([phone teleoperation](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/phone_teleop)), but it needs the PAROL6 URDF and an inverse-kinematics step.
  - Overhead camera: a [Logitech C920s](https://www.logitech.com/en-us/shop/p/c920s-pro-hd-webcam), US$69.99.
  - Wrist camera: an [innomaker U20CAM-1080P](https://www.amazon.nl/-/en/InnoMaker-Raspberry-Supports-Windows-Android/dp/B0CNCSFQC1), €18.59. A wrist mount for joint 6 is ours to design and print.
  - Camera mount: a [SmallRig Magic Arm clamp kit 4454](https://www.walmart.com/ip/SMALLRIG-Magic-Arm-Clamp-Kit-Camera-Mount-Articulating-Friction-Arm-w-Super-Clamp-w-1-4-20-Screw-Thread-hole-Camera-Light-Monitor-Webcam-Action-Camer/7297800815), US$35.90. A printed mount also works.
  - With an SO-101 leader arm built from its official bill of materials instead of the phone, L2 is roughly $250. That leader has no match for PAROL6's joint 4, so joint 4 is held.
- **L3 and up:** GPU time. The [hardware guide](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/hardware_guide) calls its numbers indicative, ±50%.
  - ACT trains in about 2 to 6 GB. The guide gives 30 to 60 minutes on a 24 GB GPU, or 6 to 14 hours on an Apple silicon Mac.
  - SmolVLA trains in about 10 to 16 GB, so it needs a CUDA GPU with 16 GB or more.
  - pi0 and pi0.5 need about 24 to 40 GB, so 40 GB or more.
  - The guide's advice for a CPU alone is not to train.
  - Hugging Face Jobs bills cloud GPUs by the second. Its prices are not yet checked.
  - Memory and speed when the policy runs are not yet measured. We measure them at L3.

The docs recommend at least 50 episodes ([imitation learning docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/il_robots)). The SmolVLA guide says 25 episodes were not enough.

## Prior art

In a search on 2026-11-11, we found nobody public running LeRobot on the stock PAROL6 board through `parol6-server`. So L1 is new work, with examples to learn from.

- **RoboCrafty's PAROL6 plugin.** [RoboCrafty/my_robot](https://github.com/RoboCrafty/my_robot/tree/996d4917279cf9f143fffac66d030cd47078eee7) runs LeRobot on a PAROL6 with a replacement controller: an ESP32 with TMC2209 drivers, and a Raspberry Pi.
  - Its plugin is also a network client of its own controller, the same shape as our plan.
  - Its [datasets and models](https://huggingface.co/RoboCrafty) are on Hugging Face. [parol6_stack_real_4](https://huggingface.co/datasets/RoboCrafty/parol6_stack_real_4) holds 100 episodes and 79,957 frames at 30 fps, from two cameras.
  - About 23 model repositories hold ACT, SmolVLA, pi0 and pi0.5 policies. The model cards make no success-rate claims.
  - The repository has no licence file, so its code is not reusable as it is.
  - It proves the arm's shape works with LeRobot. It does not prove the stock board at 100 Hz behaves the same.
- **Source Robotics' RAFT leader (in progress).** [RAFT](https://github.com/Source-Robotics/RAFT-teleoperation-arm/tree/6fe5a811422d44248013b5ed774aa74d76f3d545) is the maker's own force-feedback leader arm, designed for PAR6 and PAROL6.
  - Its geometry is PAROL6's kinematics at about 0.55×.
  - At v1.0.0, its control of PAR6 is tested in simulation and not yet on the real robot.
  - We watch it for L3 and later, and do not build on it for L1.
- **A draft plugin.** [Mateo-lab/PAROL6-x-LEROBOT](https://github.com/Mateo-lab/PAROL6-x-LEROBOT) is marked as in development. By our reading, it does not match current LeRobot or the official API. We use it only for its MuJoCo and gamepad ideas.

## Safety

A learned policy can send any target. So the driver and the person at the bench keep it slow and small.

- **Slow, capped moves.** The driver clips every target to the joint limits and caps the step from the last target. L1 jogs at 10% speed.
- **A person at the E-stop**, for every run of a policy and every test above.
- **The E-stop is a software stop.** [The board reports it](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/02-safety/emergency-stop/); `parol6-server` stops the motion. The driver then raises an error, so recording or a rollout stops. It never calls `reset()` itself; a person does.
- **A frozen LeRobot process** is caught by the server's brake after 0.25 s of silence.
- **Never cut motor power on disconnect.** The arm falls when the motors lose power, so the driver leaves the server holding the pose.
- **No lunge on connect, no drop on disconnect.** These are the failure modes of a naive driver, named by [another LeRobot plugin](https://github.com/charlie8612/lerobot_robot_piper/tree/d9785e6256aacd9fe0128a622909e046e66aea33). L1 tests both.
- **The mains switch.** A crashed computer, a frozen server or a pulled USB cable leaves the E-stop without effect. Then the board keeps executing the last packet it received. The hard stop is a switch on the mains side of the power supply, within reach; the arm falls when you use it.

PAROL6 runs [open loop](/lore/m1-bench-it). The position it reports is a step count, so a stalled joint still reports where it was told to go. A policy learns from where the arm thinks it is. After any stall, home the arm again.

## Terms

- **LeRobot**: Hugging Face's open-source library for robot learning: teleoperation, recording, datasets, training and running policies ([LeRobot](https://github.com/huggingface/lerobot/tree/e581c1d5701f846788662079d4ac08835c96def9)).
- **imitation learning**: training a robot from recorded demonstrations of a task ([imitation learning docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/il_robots)).
- **teleoperation**: a person drives the robot live from another device.
- **leader arm**: the arm a person moves during teleoperation; the robot follows it.
- **episode**: one recorded demonstration, from start to end, in a LeRobot dataset ([dataset docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/lerobot-dataset-v3)).
- **ACT**: a LeRobot policy with an image backbone and a transformer that predicts chunks of actions ([ACT docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/act)).
- **SmolVLA**: a small vision-language-action model that takes a text prompt with the cameras and joints ([SmolVLA docs](https://huggingface.co/docs/lerobot/smolvla)).
- **parol6-server**: the official PAROL6 controller program that owns the serial port and holds the safety logic ([Python API](https://docs.source-robotics.com/PAROL-docs/1.1/programming/02-python-api/)).
